{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Issachar.</b> Because of the conjunction of two shin letters, they dropped one from the pronunciation, in the manner of מְחַצְצְרִים (1 Chronicles 15:24), which is read מְחַצְרִים. And there is no shin like it in Scripture. The practice of the Hebrews when vav is conjoined with one of the lip letters — which are bet, mem, peh, the companions of vav — is that they add an alef in pronunciation and point the vav with shureq, such as וּפְרוּ וְרָבוּ (Jeremiah 20:3?) and וּמְלָאוּ בָתֵּי מִצְרַיִם (below, 8:17), and likewise וּבְנְיָמִין. Because Benjamin was the son of the mistress, they mentioned him before the sons of the maidservants."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The verse citation for ופרו ורבו appears as 'ירמי׳ כ ג' which would be Jeremiah 23:3 in the Hebrew verse numbering; rendered literally as given."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:41.048353873Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}